Commvault deduplication failure scenarios


1. Deduplication Database (DDB) Unavailable or Offline
Symptoms

Backup jobs fail or run in non-dedup mode

Errors referencing DDB access, fingerprint lookup, or hash store

MediaAgent shows DDB offline in Command Center

Common Causes

Disk hosting the DDB is full or read-only

Filesystem corruption

MediaAgent service stopped

Network path issue (if shared DDB)

Troubleshooting

Verify MediaAgent services are running

Check DDB disk space and permissions

Validate filesystem health (I/O errors, latency)

Run DDB verification from Command Center

If corrupted, perform DDB reconstruction (last resort)

Best Practice: Always place DDB on fast, dedicated disk (SSD).

2. Poor or Dropping Deduplication Ratio
Symptoms

Dedup ratio suddenly decreases

Storage growth higher than expected

More data written during backups

Common Causes

Too many dissimilar workloads in one DDB

Encrypted or compressed source data

Application-level compression (e.g., database dumps)

Frequent full backups instead of incrementals

Troubleshooting

Review workloads sharing the DDB

Separate high-change or encrypted data into another storage policy

Confirm Incremental Forever is enabled

Check job statistics for dedup savings

Design Tip: Group similar data types in the same DDB.

3. DDB Pruning or Maintenance Failures
Symptoms

DDB grows uncontrollably

Pruning jobs fail or never complete

Storage not reclaimed after data aging

Common Causes

Pruning disabled

Long incremental chains

High retention policies

MediaAgent resource contention

Troubleshooting

Verify DDB pruning schedule is enabled

Confirm data aging is occurring

Check MediaAgent CPU, memory, and disk I/O

Run pruning manually during low-usage windows

Key Point: Pruning depends on successful data aging.

4. Backup Jobs Fall Back to Non-Deduplicated Mode
Symptoms

Jobs complete but write much more data

Deduplication is bypassed silently

Storage fills unexpectedly

Common Causes

Temporary DDB lock or access failure

MediaAgent overload

Network latency to DDB disk

DDB in read-only or degraded mode

Troubleshooting

Check job logs for “fallback to non-dedup” messages

Validate MediaAgent load and disk latency

Confirm DDB is writable

Restart MediaAgent services if needed

Important: Fallback jobs increase storage usage immediately.

5. Slow Backup or Restore Performance (Dedup-Related)
Symptoms

Backups take longer than expected

Restores are slow, especially full restores

High MediaAgent CPU usage

Common Causes

DDB located on slow disk

Overloaded MediaAgent

Excessive number of streams

Large, fragmented DDB

Troubleshooting

Check MediaAgent CPU, memory, and I/O wait

Review stream count and throttling settings

Consider moving DDB to SSD

Add or load-balance MediaAgents

Restore Performance Tip: Synthetic fulls help reduce restore chain length.

6. DDB Corruption or Metadata Inconsistency
Symptoms

Repeated job failures

Hash lookup errors

Verification failures

Common Causes

Improper shutdowns

Disk or filesystem corruption

Hardware issues

Troubleshooting

Run DDB verification

Restore DDB from backup if available

Perform DDB reconstruction if restore fails

Open Commvault support case if needed

Reconstruction is resource-intensive and should be a last resort.

7. DASH Copy or Auxiliary Copy Dedup Issues
Symptoms

DASH copy jobs fail

Secondary copies consume excessive storage

Dedup not honored on secondary storage

Common Causes

Source and target using different DDBs

Network latency

Misconfigured storage policies

Troubleshooting

Confirm DASH copy configuration

Verify compatible dedup settings on source and target

Check copy job logs for block-level transfer issues

Ensure MediaAgents can communicate efficiently

Common Commvault deduplication failures include DDB unavailability, poor dedup ratios, pruning failures, fallback to non-dedup mode, and DDB corruption. Troubleshooting focuses on MediaAgent health, DDB disk performance, proper maintenance tasks, and correct workload grouping. Protecting and monitoring the DDB is critical for deduplication reliability.

